CPU     ->
        Brain of the computer, is responsible for executing instructions from programs.
        Performs basic arithmetic, logical and input/output operation specified by instructions
        EX: Intel, AMD

CORE    ->
        Individual processing units within a CPU.
        Modern CPU have multiple cores, performing multiple tasks simultaneously.

PROGRAM ->
        Set of instructions written in a programming language that tells the computer how to perform a specific task.

PROCESS ->
        Instance of a program that is being executed.
        When a program runs  the OS creates a process to manage its execution.

THREAD  ->
        A thread is a smallest unit of execution within a process.
        A process can have multiple threads, which share same resources but run independently.
        EX: A browser might use multiple threads for different tabs with each tab running as a separate thread.

MULTI TASKING ->
        It allows OS to run multiple processes simultaneously.
        On SINGLE-CORE this is done through time sharing, rapidly switching between tasks.
        On MULTI-CORE true parallel executions occurs, with task distribution across cores.
        OS scheduler balances the load, ensuring efficient and responsive system performance.

        It utilizes the capabilities of the CPU and its cores.
        When OS performs multitasking, it can assign different tasks to different cores.
        More efficient than assigning all the task to single cores.

MULTI THREADING ->
        It refers to the ability to execute multiple threads within a single process concurrently.
        Like having separate threads for rendering page, running JS and managing user inputs, making browser more responsive.

        Enhances the efficiency of multi-tasking by breaking down individual tasks into smaller sub-tasks or threads.
        Processing these threads simultaneously makes the CPU compatibility better.

SINGLE-CORE SYSTEM VS MULTI-CORE SYSTEM ->

    SINGLE-CORE SYSTEM ->
        Threads and the processes are managed by OS scheduler through time slicing and context switching.
        Creating illusion of simultaneous execution.

    MULTI-CORE SYSTEM ->
        Both threads and processes can run in true parallelism on different cores.
        With OS schedulers distributing across the cores to optimize performance.

TIME SLICING ->
        Divides time interval into small slices or quanta.
        Function: OS scheduler allocates these slices to different processes and threads, ensuring each gets a fair share of CPU time.
        Purpose: Single thread from monopolizing the CPU, improving responsiveness and enabling concurrent execution.

CONTEXT SWITCHING ->
        It is the process of saving the current state of the running process or thread and loading the next one to be executed.
        Function: When threads time slice expires, the OS scheduler performs a context switch to move the CPU focus to another process or thread.
        Purpose: Allows multiple processes and threads to share CPU giving appearance of simultaneous in single-core and improve parallelism in multi-core.


